Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero 0.11 installed-folder UEFI loader

loader.zero is an original Zero UEFI x64 application. It reuses the project's
own established GOP, memory-map and kernel-handoff implementation, with a new
bounded path and ISO reader in load-iso.zero.part. No assembler, foreign
compiler, foreign bootloader or runtime is used to implement this application.

Place ZEROX64.EFI beside system.iso in a directory readable by the UEFI
firmware. Launch ZEROX64.EFI through the firmware's existing file browser or
an already configured firmware boot entry. The application obtains its own
device and UTF-16 file path from EFI_LOADED_IMAGE_PROTOCOL, replaces only its
filename with system.iso, and opens that sibling using firmware Simple File
System. The loader's filename and directory name are not hardcoded. Normally
the readable filesystem is FAT on an EFI system partition or a test volume.

Native preparation example (Linux or Windows toolkit):
  zero-setup deploy zero.iso ZEROX64.EFI NEW_DIRECTORY
  zero-setup status NEW_DIRECTORY
Windows uses zero-setup.exe. This creates an owned folder; it does not copy it
to a system partition, install a firmware entry or change existing boot files.
The prepared folder can be inspected before a user chooses boot integration.

The loader supports the current exact 18890752-byte Zero ISO format. It reads
16KiB of bounded metadata at byte32768 and verifies the PVD identity, volume
length, block size, root location, El Torito catalog checksum/entries and the
paired-endian kernel/tools directory extents. Each selected member must occur
exactly once. Unsupported ISO layouts and malformed entries are rejected.
It then reads the x64 kernel directly from ISO byte81920 (475136 bytes; legacy491520-byte slot also accepted) and
the native compiler bundle from byte17842176 (1048576 bytes). Partial firmware
reads are completed; premature EOF, oversized reads, any EFI warning/error,
bad tool-bundle magic/version/length/count or failed seek stops before firmware
exit. The complete ISO is not copied into RAM and no ISO loop device is used.

Successful boot uses the same original kernel, compiler bundle, 1080p GOP
selection, allocated stack/workspace, checked memory map and bounded
ExitBootServices retry path as the ordinary optical UEFI loader. Existing
BIOS and IA32 UEFI sources and the optical ISO are unchanged by this loader.
After firmware exit, the kernel uses its normal hardware drivers.

Limits and unfinished installation features
- UEFI x64 only. This additional loader does not add BIOS directory boot,
  IA32 directory boot, Secure Boot signing or a universal host filesystem.
- A Linux ext4/Btrfs or Windows NTFS directory is not automatically readable
  by firmware. No new ext4/NTFS/Btrfs early driver is supplied here.
- storage.data remains an ordinary host file. The kernel does not map it as
  a writable block device; this loader does not claim installed persistence.
  A separately attached physical/virtual data disk is a different mechanism.
- No BCD, GRUB, firmware boot entry, boot sector or partition table is changed.
  Full Wubi-style installation and uninstall boot-menu rollback remain open.
- Metadata validation is structural, not publisher authentication or complete
  payload integrity verification. The ISO must be trusted. Firmware Secure
  Boot is not implemented by this unsigned application.
- Loaded-image device paths are limited to4096 bytes and accepted FILEPATH
  strings to2036 UTF-16 code units. Only ordinary FILEPATH nodes followed by
  an END_ENTIRE node are accepted; ambiguous device paths fail closed. The
  generated sibling path fits an owned4096-byte UTF-16 buffer.
- Failure halts before ExitBootServices and emits a serial stage diagnostic.
  A graphical recovery menu and unattended recovery are not implemented.

Build and verification
  python3 installer/boot/build.py
  python3 installer/boot/test-vm.py
Those scripts are external host orchestration, not delivered runtime logic.
build.py invokes the project's own compiler --efi directly. The actual binary
is installer/boot/build/ZEROX64.EFI; its source is loader.zero.

The QEMU/OVMF fixture uses a private FAT volume containing EFI/BOOT/system.iso
and the loader renamed to the standard EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI fallback name.
No CD-ROM is attached. The native deploy tool creates that folder when it is
available; the fixture adds only the fallback filename for firmware discovery.
The real unmodified kernel reaches its terminal and executes Zero/Ezero from
the ISO bundle. Five separate malformed ISO cases stop before firmware exit.
Original data.img/data.vdi/zero.iso are hash-checked and never attached for
writing. The private FAT backing is read-only with a private QEMU snapshot.

review/check-parser.py independently compiles the exact authored Zero path and ISO
parser blocks and exercises valid/invalid UTF-16 paths, multiple nodes,
capacity edges, buffer canaries, metadata lengths and paired-endian extents.
These are host parser tests, not a claim of booting native Windows/VirtualBox.
Verification files distinguish actual firmware tests and native parser tests.
